  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a caller granted on project P, when they POST /fetch for sprk_document selecting sprk_graphdriveid, sprk_graphitemid or sprk_filepath, then the request is refused 400 with the new column errorCode and Dataverse is not queried.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A FetchXML with `all-attributes` on any module is refused 400 with the column errorCode.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A non-allow-listed column used ONLY in a `condition`, ONLY in an `order`, or ONLY as an aliased/aggregate attribute is refused 400 — each position has its own test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A FetchXML that uses only allow-listed attributes is admitted and returns the same scoped rows as before this task (regression).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The guard order is preserved: a cross-entity FetchXML still reports `DV_FETCHXML_ENTITY_MISMATCH`, and a link-entity FetchXML still reports `DV_FETCHXML_LINK_ENTITY_NOT_PERMITTED`, even when it also names a forbidden column.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">GET /record with a `$select` naming a non-allow-listed column is refused 400; with an allow-listed `$select` or no `$select` it returns 200 with only allow-listed keys.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Defence in depth: when the executed result contains a key not on the allow-list (test double returns an extra attribute and an extra `@formattedValues` entry), both are stripped from the response and `@logicalName` survives.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Registration refuses (throws at startup) a descriptor with no allow-list, one missing a scope-dimension attribute, one missing the /record default columns, and one containing a pointer column. Every registration present at execution time (seven today; ten if task 056 has landed) declares an allow-list and the application starts.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Authorization negative: a caller with an empty accessible set submitting an allow-listed FetchXML still receives 0 rows without a Dataverse query (existing behaviour, `ExternalModuleDataEndpoints.cs:185-192`); the same caller submitting a non-allow-listed column receives the same 400 as any other caller, because the guard runs before scoping exactly as today (`:169-172`). Neither path queries Dataverse (asserted on the test double).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The internal /api/dataverse fetch and record routes are byte-for-byte unaffected (their existing tests pass unmodified).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: removing the column signal, removing the post-execution strip, or removing the alias check each turns at least one test green-to-red. All reverted before completion.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">MANUAL live gate (dev, no CI): signed in as an existing CIAM test contact and as an existing non-admin workforce test user, each of the six outside-counsel grids renders the same columns and rows as before the change, and a hand-crafted /fetch for sprk_graphitemid returns 400. Evidence (screenshots or response captures) recorded in the task notes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The allow-list derivation (grid configuration id → columns) is recorded in the task notes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full BFF unit suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported (both absolute sizes, delta, zip tool, file counts) and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above (each FetchXML position, /record, stripping, registration refusals, guard order, empty set). Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in the task notes.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Defect C6 (notes/session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md § C6; UC3 caveat "OVER-EXPOSURE"). Impact is identifier and internal-field disclosure within already-granted scope — no cross-scope rows — which is why it ranks medium, but it breaks the broker-only invariant that every other external route keeps. Investigation gap G-10 was filed under the generic "field-level visibility out of scope" deferral (spec.md:27; design-register.md D-3), whose premise ("the SPA won't ask") fails when the caller authors the FetchXML. This task closes G-10; record that in the design register's D-3 entry at completion (a notes edit, not a spec change).

Independent of tasks 135-137 in logic, but parallel-unsafe under the hot-file rule; run it before or after them, not beside. Task 136 relies on the module scope dimensions reading `CallerPrincipal` views and deliberately does not edit this task's files. Task 056 (child modules) must declare allow-lists for its three descriptors if it lands after this task — the main session should add that line to 056 (not edited here). After the criterion-8 follow-up, 056's descriptors must ALSO declare PrimaryNameAttribute (read from live EntityDefinitions) and add their entities to the LivePrimaryNames pin table.

🔔 OWNER QUESTION (executor follow-up D1, open — no client code changed for it): the external grids mount the shared DataGrid with the default showViewSelector=true (src/client/external-spa/src/widgets/GridWidgetBody.tsx), so CIAM users can pick the entity's INTERNAL MDA main views from /savedqueries/{entity}. That is why each allow-list includes columns from sibling main views that render rows today (e.g. ownerid, sprk_assignedto, sprk_visibilitystate). Should external grids hide the ViewSelector (showViewSelector=false)? If yes, every allow-list can shrink to grid config + scope attributes + /record default projection, removing those internal columns from external reach; if no, the allow-lists stay as derived and any change to an internal main view's columns must also be mirrored in ReadableColumns. Owner decision required before either change.</notes>

